1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:05,067
A workflow does work for you when something happens on your site.

2
00:00:05,067 --> 00:00:09,077
In this video, we build one that emails the editors each time an

3
00:00:09,077 --> 00:00:13,430
article is published, using the Workflows, Contents and Email modules.

4
00:00:18,030 --> 00:00:21,423
Editors often need to know when an article goes live.

5
00:00:21,423 --> 00:00:26,772
Without automation, someone has to notice it, then write and send each message by hand.

6
00:00:26,772 --> 00:00:28,900
A workflow can do that for you.

7
00:00:29,650 --> 00:00:31,838
A workflow connects activities.

8
00:00:31,838 --> 00:00:36,686
An event waits for something to happen, such as content being published, and

9
00:00:36,686 --> 00:00:39,879
a task does something, such as sending an email.

10
00:00:39,879 --> 00:00:45,200
An outcome says how an activity ended, such as Done, and leads to the next activity.

11
00:00:45,950 --> 00:00:47,662
Ours has two activities.

12
00:00:47,662 --> 00:00:52,456
Content Published Event starts the workflow, and Email Task sends the message.

13
00:00:52,456 --> 00:00:55,310
The Done outcome of the event connects them.

14
00:00:59,510 --> 00:01:04,390
The site needs the Workflows and Email features and an email provider, all

15
00:01:04,390 --> 00:01:06,586
enabled under Tools, Features.

16
00:01:06,586 --> 00:01:09,696
Here, SMTP Email Provider sends the messages.

17
00:01:09,696 --> 00:01:14,271
The Contents feature is enabled on this site, and it adds the content

18
00:01:14,271 --> 00:01:16,650
events when Workflows is enabled.

19
00:01:18,339 --> 00:01:22,675
The email settings are under Settings, Communication, Email.

20
00:01:22,675 --> 00:01:27,538
On the SMTP tab, Enable SMTP Provider is checked, and a Sender email

21
00:01:27,538 --> 00:01:31,020
address, a Host name and a Port number are set.

22
00:01:31,020 --> 00:01:33,649
A separate video covers this page.

23
00:01:34,113 --> 00:01:39,359
The messages go to a test mail server on this computer, so nothing leaves it.

24
00:01:39,359 --> 00:01:43,973
Its inbox is empty, and each email the workflow sends appears here.

25
00:01:48,187 --> 00:01:50,003
Open Design, Workflows.

26
00:01:50,003 --> 00:01:54,887
The list is empty, so click Create Workflow to start the first workflow.

27
00:01:55,352 --> 00:01:56,946
The form only needs a Name.

28
00:01:56,946 --> 00:02:00,762
The settings under More settings have defaults that you can change later.

29
00:02:00,762 --> 00:02:04,820
Let's type "Notify the editors when an article is published", and click Create

30
00:02:04,820 --> 00:02:06,172
and open the designer.

31
00:02:10,383 --> 00:02:15,124
The designer opens with the list of Activities on the left and an empty canvas.

32
00:02:15,124 --> 00:02:19,809
A workflow runs when something happens, so the canvas offers the common events

33
00:02:19,809 --> 00:02:21,593
under Start with an event.

34
00:02:22,170 --> 00:02:24,063
Click Content Published Event.

35
00:02:24,063 --> 00:02:27,850
It lands on the canvas with a Start badge, because the first event

36
00:02:27,850 --> 00:02:29,902
added becomes the start activity.

37
00:02:29,902 --> 00:02:31,480
Its settings open below.

38
00:02:31,974 --> 00:02:35,694
Content Types limits the event to some types of content.

39
00:02:35,694 --> 00:02:40,494
With none selected, any type starts the workflow, so let's select Article.

40
00:02:40,494 --> 00:02:43,254
The card on the canvas shows the choice.

41
00:02:47,449 --> 00:02:49,835
Click the Done outcome of the event.

42
00:02:49,835 --> 00:02:53,016
The menu offers the activities that can come next.

43
00:02:53,016 --> 00:02:55,629
Search for email, and choose Email Task.

44
00:02:56,129 --> 00:03:00,260
The Email Task is added next to the event, connected from Done, and

45
00:03:00,260 --> 00:03:01,957
its settings open below.

46
00:03:01,957 --> 00:03:05,749
Drag the top edge of the panel up to give the form more room.

47
00:03:06,219 --> 00:03:11,423
Leave From, Sender and ReplyTo empty, so the email uses the Sender email

48
00:03:11,423 --> 00:03:13,758
address of the SMTP settings.

49
00:03:13,758 --> 00:03:16,960
In Recipients, type "editors@example.com".

50
00:03:16,960 --> 00:03:20,429
A comma-separated list reaches several people.

51
00:03:20,891 --> 00:03:24,062
In Subject, type "An article was published".

52
00:03:24,062 --> 00:03:28,691
Then set Format to Text, so the email has a single plain text body.

53
00:03:29,175 --> 00:03:31,322
In Text Body, type a short message.

54
00:03:31,322 --> 00:03:35,041
The changes are applied when you leave a field, or when you click

55
00:03:35,041 --> 00:03:37,555
Apply, and the button confirms with Saved!

56
00:03:38,033 --> 00:03:42,517
Back on the canvas, the Email Task shows its subject and two outcomes.

57
00:03:42,517 --> 00:03:47,297
Done follows a sent message, and Failed follows one that could not be sent.

58
00:03:47,297 --> 00:03:51,133
Neither is connected, so the workflow ends after the email.

59
00:03:55,407 --> 00:04:00,094
The designer saves every change to a draft, and a draft does not run.

60
00:04:00,094 --> 00:04:03,907
When the toolbar says All changes saved, click Publish.

61
00:04:04,365 --> 00:04:08,654
A message confirms that the workflow is published, and a badge next to

62
00:04:08,654 --> 00:04:10,516
its name shows the version.

63
00:04:10,516 --> 00:04:14,015
From here on, publishing an article starts the workflow.

64
00:04:14,489 --> 00:04:16,425
Each publish creates a version.

65
00:04:16,425 --> 00:04:20,086
When you edit the workflow later, the changes go to a draft, and

66
00:04:20,086 --> 00:04:23,539
the published version keeps running until you publish again.

67
00:04:27,764 --> 00:04:29,624
Let's publish an article.

68
00:04:29,624 --> 00:04:34,004
Under Content, Content Items, open the New menu and choose Article.

69
00:04:34,464 --> 00:04:35,559
Type a Title.

70
00:04:35,559 --> 00:04:40,512
The workflow waits for content to be published, so Save Draft does not start it.

71
00:04:40,512 --> 00:04:41,664
Click Publish.

72
00:04:42,121 --> 00:04:44,930
A message confirms that the article is published.

73
00:04:44,930 --> 00:04:46,921
The workflow runs at that moment.

74
00:04:47,383 --> 00:04:52,979
Back under Design, Workflows, the Instances button lists the runs of every workflow.

75
00:04:52,979 --> 00:04:56,523
Ours has the status Finished, so it ran to its end.

76
00:04:56,973 --> 00:05:00,070
In the test mail server, the email has arrived.

77
00:05:00,070 --> 00:05:05,328
It comes from the Sender email address, goes to editors@example.com, and carries our

78
00:05:05,328 --> 00:05:07,723
subject and our plain text message.

79
00:05:11,923 --> 00:05:16,309
To adapt the workflow, select more content types in the event, or none

80
00:05:16,309 --> 00:05:18,213
to start it for every type.

81
00:05:18,963 --> 00:05:24,850
Content Created Event, Content Updated Event and Content Unpublished Event start a workflow

82
00:05:24,850 --> 00:05:27,823
in the same way as Content Published Event.

83
00:05:28,573 --> 00:05:33,849
Under Recipients, the Cc and Bcc fields take lists of addresses in the

84
00:05:33,849 --> 00:05:36,903
same way, for copies and blind copies.

85
00:05:37,653 --> 00:05:42,007
The Subject and Text Body fields can use values from the content item,

86
00:05:42,007 --> 00:05:43,382
such as its title.

87
00:05:43,382 --> 00:05:46,533
The Available data tab of an activity lists them.

88
00:05:48,533 --> 00:05:53,003
A workflow joins Content Published Event to Email Task, so the editors get

89
00:05:53,003 --> 00:05:55,741
an email each time an article is published.

90
00:05:55,741 --> 00:05:57,193
Thanks for watching.

